
Keyhole v1.0.0 will be installed as follows.

  Container    200 (keyhole), unprivileged Debian 12
  Resources    2 cores, 1024 MB RAM, 8 GB on local-lvm, bridge vmbr0
  Reached by   tls
  Reached at   https://<the container's address>:8477
  Binary       https://github.com/ssan9876/keyhole/releases/download/v1.0.0/keyhole-linux-${arch}
               verified against SHA256SUMS and its minisign signature
  Data         /var/lib/keyhole (SQLite; entirely ciphertext)
  Config       /etc/keyhole/config.yml
  Service      systemd unit "keyhole", running as keyhole
  Admin        me@example.com

Nothing has been changed yet.

!! The release signing key in this copy of the script is still a placeholder,
!! so it can verify nothing. A real install refuses to start until
!! MINISIGN_PUBKEY is replaced with the key published in the README.
RUN: pveam update
RUN: sh -c pveam available --section system | awk '{print $2}' | grep '^debian-12-standard' | sort -V | tail -n 1
  the newest debian-12-standard in that list is the template; it is downloaded if absent
RUN: pveam download local <newest debian-12-standard>
RUN: pct create 200 local:vztmpl/<newest debian-12-standard> --hostname keyhole --cores 2 --memory 1024 --swap 0 --rootfs local-lvm:8 --net0 name=eth0,bridge=vmbr0,ip=dhcp --unprivileged 1 --features nesting=0 --onboot 1
RUN: pct start 200
RUN: sh -c i=0; while [ $i -lt 60 ]; do pct exec 200 -- true >/dev/null 2>&1 && exit 0; i=$((i+1)); sleep 1; done; exit 1
RUN: pct exec 200 -- sh -c set -eu
export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
apt-get update -qq
apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends ca-certificates curl minisign openssl
RUN: pct exec 200 -- sh -c set -eu
rm -rf '/tmp/keyhole-install'
mkdir -p '/tmp/keyhole-install'
cd '/tmp/keyhole-install'
arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture)
curl -fsSL --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -o "keyhole-linux-$arch" "https://github.com/ssan9876/keyhole/releases/download/v1.0.0/keyhole-linux-$arch"
curl -fsSL --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -o SHA256SUMS "https://github.com/ssan9876/keyhole/releases/download/v1.0.0/SHA256SUMS"
curl -fsSL --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -o SHA256SUMS.minisig "https://github.com/ssan9876/keyhole/releases/download/v1.0.0/SHA256SUMS.minisig"
RUN: pct exec 200 -- sh -c set -eu
cd '/tmp/keyhole-install'
minisign -Vm SHA256SUMS -P 'RWQ...replace-with-the-real-key...'
RUN: pct exec 200 -- sh -c set -eu
cd '/tmp/keyhole-install'
sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS --ignore-missing
RUN: pct exec 200 -- sh -c set -eu
cd '/tmp/keyhole-install'
arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture)
install -o root -g root -m 0755 "keyhole-linux-$arch" /usr/local/bin/keyhole
rm -rf '/tmp/keyhole-install'
RUN: pct exec 200 -- sh -c set -eu
umask 077
cat > '/usr/local/bin/update' <<'KEYHOLE_EOF'
#!/bin/sh
exec /usr/local/bin/keyhole update "$@"
KEYHOLE_EOF
chown 'root:root' '/usr/local/bin/update'
chmod '0755' '/usr/local/bin/update'
RUN: pct exec 200 -- sh -c set -eu
id -u 'keyhole' >/dev/null 2>&1 || useradd --system --home-dir '/var/lib/keyhole' --shell /usr/sbin/nologin 'keyhole'
RUN: pct exec 200 -- install -d -o keyhole -g keyhole -m 0700 /var/lib/keyhole
RUN: pct exec 200 -- install -d -o root -g root -m 0755 /etc/keyhole
RUN: pct exec 200 -- sh -c set -eu
ip=$(hostname -I | cut -d' ' -f1)
[ -n "$ip" ] || { echo 'the container has no IPv4 address yet' >&2; exit 1; }
umask 077
openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes -days 3650 -subj "/CN=$ip" -addext "subjectAltName=IP:$ip" -keyout '/etc/keyhole/tls.key' -out '/etc/keyhole/tls.crt'
chown root:keyhole '/etc/keyhole/tls.key' '/etc/keyhole/tls.crt'
chmod 0640 '/etc/keyhole/tls.key' '/etc/keyhole/tls.crt'
RUN: pct exec 200 -- openssl x509 -noout -fingerprint -sha256 -in /etc/keyhole/tls.crt
RUN: pct exec 200 -- sh -c set -eu
ip=$(hostname -I | cut -d' ' -f1)
# An empty $ip here would write "base_url: https://:8477" and produce a vault
# whose setup links point nowhere, with nothing to say why. configure_network
# already fails on this, so reaching it means the two were reordered.
[ -n "$ip" ] || { echo 'the container has no IPv4 address yet' >&2; exit 1; }
umask 077
cat > '/etc/keyhole/config.yml' <<EOF
# Written by scripts/install.sh (Keyhole v1.0.0).
addr: 0.0.0.0:8477
data_dir: /var/lib/keyhole
base_url: https://$ip:8477
log_level: info
tls_cert: /etc/keyhole/tls.crt
tls_key: /etc/keyhole/tls.key
EOF
chown 'root:keyhole' '/etc/keyhole/config.yml'
chmod 0640 '/etc/keyhole/config.yml'
RUN: pct exec 200 -- sh -c set -eu
umask 077
cat > '/etc/systemd/system/keyhole.service' <<'KEYHOLE_EOF'
[Unit]
Description=Keyhole password manager
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=simple
User=keyhole
Group=keyhole
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/keyhole serve --config /etc/keyhole/config.yml
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=2

# The process needs one writable directory and nothing else. Everything below
# is what keeps a compromise of the HTTP handler from becoming a compromise of
# the container.
NoNewPrivileges=yes
PrivateTmp=yes
PrivateDevices=yes
ProtectSystem=strict
ProtectHome=yes
ProtectKernelTunables=yes
ProtectKernelModules=yes
ProtectControlGroups=yes
RestrictSUIDSGID=yes
RestrictNamespaces=yes
LockPersonality=yes
# Safe here, and worth keeping: the binary is CGO_ENABLED=0 static Go with no
# JIT and no plugins, so nothing it does needs a page that is both writable and
# executable. If something later appears to break under it, that is a reason to
# find out what is allocating W+X memory, not a reason to delete this line.
MemoryDenyWriteExecute=yes
SystemCallFilter=@system-service
SystemCallArchitectures=native
ReadWritePaths=/var/lib/keyhole

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
KEYHOLE_EOF
chown 'root:root' '/etc/systemd/system/keyhole.service'
chmod '0644' '/etc/systemd/system/keyhole.service'
RUN: pct exec 200 -- systemctl daemon-reload
RUN: pct exec 200 -- keyhole migrate --config /etc/keyhole/config.yml
RUN: pct exec 200 -- chown -R keyhole:keyhole /var/lib/keyhole
RUN: pct exec 200 -- systemctl enable --now keyhole
RUN: sh -c i=0; while [ $i -lt 30 ]; do pct exec 200 -- curl -fsSk -o /dev/null 'https://127.0.0.1:8477/healthz' >/dev/null 2>&1 && exit 0; i=$((i+1)); sleep 1; done; exit 1
RUN: pct exec 200 -- runuser -u keyhole -- keyhole admin create --email me@example.com --config /etc/keyhole/config.yml

That is the entire plan for Keyhole v1.0.0 in container 200. Nothing was changed.
The setup link is printed by "keyhole admin create" at the end of a real run.

The certificate is self-signed, so the browser warns the first time. Check the
fingerprint it shows you against this one rather than clicking through:

  (read from the certificate once it has been generated)

Worth knowing:

  Data          /var/lib/keyhole inside container 200 — item bodies, names and
                URLs are ciphertext the server has never held a key for
  Back up       pct exec 200 -- keyhole backup
                (a snapshot is entirely ciphertext, so copying it somewhere
                less trusted than this host is a reasonable thing to do)
  Update        pct exec 200 -- update
                (verifies the signature the same way this install did, and
                rolls back if the new binary will not answer /healthz)
  Logs          pct exec 200 -- journalctl -u keyhole -f
